Assignment 4 Dependency Note 
R: 
tidyverse: this function uses a collection of R packages which is used to import, clean, organize, and analyze the NHANES dataset.
readr: Uses read_csv() to load the NHANES CSV file into R.
dplyr: Uses filter() to remove missing BMI, IncomeGroup, and Gender values and group_by() to organize observations by income and gender, then the summarise() to calculate grouped BMI statistics, and arrange() to sort the results.
Base R: Uses mean() and sd() to calculate BMI statistics, round() to round results to two decimal places, is.na() to identify missing values, and nrow() and ncol() to verify dataset dimensions.
Python: 
pandas: Used to reproduce the R analysis in Python.
read_csv() loads the NHANES dataset.
dropna() removes observations with missing values.
groupby() organizes observations by IncomeGroup and Gender.
agg() calculates group counts, mean BMI, and standard deviations.
round() rounds results to two decimal places.
ipykernel: Provides the Python kernel used to execute the code cells in translation.ipynb.
helpers.py: this contained  the reusable summarize_mean_sd() function. The function is imported and called in translation.ipynb to reproduce the grouped BMI summary.

Both R and Python use the same NHANES dataset, missing-value filters, grouping variables, and summary calculations. Relative file paths allow the analyses to access the dataset within the repository.
The parity table confirms that both languages produce matching row counts, grouping labels, and BMI statistics rounded to two decimal places.